diff --git a/Tools/assert_details/README.rst b/Tools/assert_details/README.rst
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..fe5db272c6c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Tools/assert_details/README.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,122 @@
+Native assertion diagnostics prototype
+======================================
+
+The CPython compiler captures assertion values. The public ``assertionlib`` module
+provides expression graphs, formatters, and context-local callbacks.
+See ``Doc/library/assertionlib.rst`` for the API contract and compiler flags.
+
+The proposed exception pickle policy still needs implementation work.
+
+Build and run
+-------------
+
+Build CPython with the normal configure and make commands. Use the release
+build for latency measurements::
+
+    ./python -X assert_details Tools/assert_details/demo.py
+
+The example exits with an AssertionError and a detailed traceback.
+The default explanation substitutes captured operands into the assertion.
+Calls and attributes add nested ``where`` lines. The structured API retains
+all capture data. This includes skipped nodes and recorded truth results.
+
+Capture is off by default. ``-O`` and ``-OO`` remove assertions.
+Capture uses a separate bytecode cache tag.
+
+``Tools/assert_details/run.py`` still provides capture for one selected file.
+The compiler flags apply to source and AST input. Flags do not affect code
+that already exists. Frozen modules retain their original behavior.
+
+Capture and cleanup
+-------------------
+
+Each capture uses a compiler-only fast local. Frames initialize these slots
+to NULL. A slot remains NULL when short circuit evaluation skips its expression.
+The compiler reuses slots between assertions. Comparison nodes use the result
+of their last evaluated link. Constant true assertions emit no capture code.
+
+Capture uses ``STORE_FAST slot; LOAD_FAST slot``. Cleanup uses
+``LOAD_FAST_AND_CLEAR slot; POP_TOP`` on success and on exceptions.
+These existing instructions also handle unset slots.
+Hidden slots do not appear in ``locals()``.
+
+The constant plan stores expression text, source spans, and child nodes once.
+Each capture record identifies a slot, a kind, and a node.
+Comparison records also identify the operator and operand nodes.
+
+The compiler captures actual truth results. It does not repeat truth conversion.
+Calls, attributes, slices, arithmetic, conditional expressions, assignment
+expressions, and containers use the same capture path. Comprehensions and
+generator expressions expose their result as one value.
+
+Method-call and builtin-call shortcuts can bypass expression values.
+Capture disables those shortcuts where necessary. This still contributes to
+the measured overhead. The implementation adds no opcodes or optimizer rules.
+
+An intrinsic builds the payload on failure. The public details object is lazy.
+Failure callbacks can replace the exception message before the caller catches it.
+With no callback, ``AssertionError.args`` and ``str(error)`` retain their
+normal behavior. Successful assertions in ordinary diagnostic mode create no
+diagnostic containers and perform no callback lookup.
+
+Captured references extend object lifetimes and do not freeze mutable state.
+Use ``assertionlib.clear_details(error)`` to detach them from an exception.
+A retained details object still owns its values. The private capture payload
+has no stable serialization contract.
+
+Pytest adapter
+--------------
+
+The experimental adapter keeps pytest's import selection, module registration,
+comparison hooks, and report code. It replaces its source-to-code entry point.
+It compiles selected modules with ``optimize=0`` and uses a separate cache tag.
+It supports failure callbacks. It does not promise exact pytest explanation text.
+
+Add this directory, the pytest source directory, and its dependencies to
+``PYTHONPATH``. Then run::
+
+    ./python -m pytest -p pytest_native path/to/tests
+
+Exercise the adapter and its hooks::
+
+    ./python Tools/assert_details/pytest_smoke.py \
+        --pytest-source /path/to/pytest \
+        --pytest-deps /path/to/site-packages \
+        --output /tmp/assert-pytest
+
+Measurements
+------------
+
+Run the microbenchmarks::
+
+    ./python Tools/assert_details/benchmark.py \
+        --output /tmp/assert-results --iterations 1000 --cpu 17
+
+For a pytest comparison, pass ``--pytest-source`` and ``--pytest-deps``.
+Add ``--callbacks`` to measure failure callback latency.
+The benchmark uses one executable for all modes. It warms each function,
+randomizes mode order, and reports raw samples, medians, and median absolute
+deviations. Runtime includes loop and harness overhead.
+
+Run without ``--iterations`` to measure one assertion per function call.
+This also gives bytecode sizes for the smallest function in each case.
+Record the compiler flags, CPU, and host load with every result.
+Use the same CPU and build options for comparisons between interpreters.
+
+Measure whole-module size and compile latency::
+
+    ./python Tools/assert_details/corpus.py --output /tmp/assert-corpus.json
+
+The corpus includes tracked production library modules with assertions.
+Its bytecode and marshal totals include code without assertions.
+
+Checks
+------
+
+Run the focused checks::
+
+    ./python -m test test_assert_details
+
+Use a debug build for reference-leak checks. Run the compiler, traceback,
+frame, import, generator, coroutine, and marshal suites after changes to
+capture or cleanup. Run the C import checks after cache-tag changes.
diff --git a/Tools/assert_details/benchmark.py b/Tools/assert_details/benchmark.py
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..c12b001423c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Tools/assert_details/benchmark.py
@@ -0,0 +1,243 @@
+"""Measure native assertion captures against plain and pytest assertions.
+
+Run with the prototype interpreter. Timed code uses compile() flags directly,
+so all modes share the same executable, library, CPU, and optimization level.
+"""
+
+import argparse
+import ast
+import dis
+import gc
+import io
+import json
+import marshal
+import os
+from pathlib import Path
+import random
+import statistics
+import sys
+import sysconfig
+import time
+import timeit
+import traceback
+import types
+
+
+CASES = {
+    'constant': ('True', ()),
+    'equal_int': ('a == b', (123, 123)),
+    'equal_large_int': ('a == b', (10**50, int(str(10**50)))),
+    'arithmetic_large_int': ('a * 2 + b == expected', (10**50, 10**49, 21 * 10**49)),
+    'arithmetic_float': ('a * 2 + b == expected', (2.25, 5.5, 10.0)),
+    'arithmetic': ('a * 2 + b == expected', (11, 20, 42)),
+    'attribute': ('a.value == b', (types.SimpleNamespace(value=42), 42)),
+    'subscript': ('a["value"] == b', ({'value': 42}, 42)),
+    'call': ('normalize(a) == b', (' ALICE ', 'alice')),
+    'method': ('a.strip().lower() == b', (' ALICE ', 'alice')),
+    'boolean_and': ('a > 0 and b > 0 and expected > 0', (1, 2, 3)),
+    'boolean_or_short': ('a > 0 or b > 0', (1, 2)),
+    'comparison_chain': ('0 < a < b < expected', (1, 2, 3)),
+    'list_equal': ('a == b', (list(range(32)), list(range(32)))),
+    'generator_all': ('all(x >= 0 for x in a)', (list(range(12)),)),
+}
+
+
+def build(expression, mode, rewrite=None, iterations=1):
+    source = ('def normalize(value):\n    return value.strip().lower()\n\n'
+              'def check(a=None, b=None, expected=None):\n'
+              + (f'    assert {expression}\n' if iterations == 1 else
+                 f'    for _ in range({iterations}):\n        assert {expression}\n'))
+    tree = ast.parse(source)
+    if mode == 'pytest':
+        rewrite(tree, source.encode())
+    flags = ast.PyCF_ASSERT_DETAILS if mode == 'native' else 0
+    code = compile(tree, '<benchmark>', 'exec', flags=flags, dont_inherit=True)
+    ns = {}
+    exec(code, ns)
+    return ns['check']
+
+
+def sizes(code):
+    codes = [code]
+    for constant in code.co_consts:
+        if isinstance(constant, types.CodeType):
+            codes.extend(walk_codes(constant))
+    return {
+        'bytecode_bytes': sum(len(c.co_code) for c in codes),
+        'instructions': sum(len(list(dis.get_instructions(c))) for c in codes),
+        'cache_units': sum(sum(i.opname == 'CACHE'
+                              for i in dis.get_instructions(c, show_caches=True))
+                           for c in codes),
+        'exception_table_bytes': sum(len(c.co_exceptiontable) for c in codes),
+        'location_table_bytes': sum(len(c.co_linetable) for c in codes),
+        'marshal_bytes': len(marshal.dumps(code)),
+        'frame_slots': code.co_nlocals + code.co_stacksize,
+        'locals': code.co_nlocals,
+        'stacksize': code.co_stacksize,
+    }
+
+
+def walk_codes(code):
+    yield code
+    for constant in code.co_consts:
+        if isinstance(constant, types.CodeType):
+            yield from walk_codes(constant)
+
+
+def calibrate(timer, minimum):
+    number = 1000
+    while True:
+        elapsed = timer.timeit(number)
+        if elapsed >= minimum / 3:
+            return max(1000, int(number * minimum / elapsed))
+        number *= 5
+
+
+def pin_idle_cpu():
+    if not hasattr(os, 'sched_getaffinity'):
+        return None
+    allowed = os.sched_getaffinity(0)
+    def counters():
+        result = {}
+        for line in Path('/proc/stat').read_text().splitlines():
+            name, *fields = line.split()
+            if name.startswith('cpu') and name[3:].isdigit():
+                cpu = int(name[3:])
+                values = list(map(int, fields))
+                result[cpu] = (sum(values[:8]), values[3] + values[4])
+        return result
+    first = counters()
+    time.sleep(0.5)
+    second = counters()
+    busy = {cpu: 1 - (second[cpu][1] - first[cpu][1]) /
+            max(1, second[cpu][0] - first[cpu][0]) for cpu in allowed}
+    cpu = min(busy, key=busy.get)
+    os.sched_setaffinity(0, {cpu})
+    return cpu
+
+
+def main():
+    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
+    parser.add_argument('--output', type=Path, required=True)
+    parser.add_argument('--pytest-source', type=Path)
+    parser.add_argument('--pytest-deps', type=Path)
+    parser.add_argument('--iterations', type=int, default=1)
+    parser.add_argument('--cpu', type=int)
+    parser.add_argument('--callbacks', action='store_true')
+    parser.add_argument('--cases', nargs='+', choices=CASES)
+    parser.add_argument('--rounds', type=int, default=9)
+    parser.add_argument('--sample-seconds', type=float, default=0.15)
+    args = parser.parse_args()
+    args.output.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
+    modes = ['plain', 'native']
+    rewrite = None
+    if args.pytest_source:
+        sys.path.insert(0, str(args.pytest_source / 'src'))
+        if args.pytest_deps:
+            sys.path.append(str(args.pytest_deps))
+        from _pytest.assertion.rewrite import rewrite_asserts
+        rewrite = rewrite_asserts
+        modes.append('pytest')
+    if args.cpu is None:
+        cpu = pin_idle_cpu()
+    else:
+        cpu = args.cpu
+        os.sched_setaffinity(0, {cpu})
+    results = {
+        'python': sys.version,
+        'executable': sys.executable,
+        'config_args': sysconfig.get_config_var('CONFIG_ARGS'),
+        'cflags': sysconfig.get_config_var('CFLAGS'),
+        'cpu': cpu,
+        'loadavg': os.getloadavg(),
+        'rounds': args.rounds,
+        'iterations_per_call': args.iterations,
+        'sample_seconds': args.sample_seconds,
+        'cases': {},
+    }
+    disassembly = io.StringIO()
+    rng = random.Random(20261018)
+    for name, (expression, call_args) in CASES.items():
+        if args.cases and name not in args.cases:
+            continue
+        case = results['cases'][name] = {'expression': expression, 'modes': {}}
+        timers = {}
+        for mode in modes:
+            fn = build(expression, mode, rewrite, args.iterations)
+            for _ in range(max(5, 5000 // args.iterations)):
+                fn(*call_args)
+            timer = timeit.Timer('fn(*call_args)', globals={'fn': fn, 'call_args': call_args})
+            count = calibrate(timer, args.sample_seconds)
+            timers[mode] = (timer, count)
+            case['modes'][mode] = {'sizes': sizes(fn.__code__), 'samples_ns': [], 'loops': count}
+            print(f'\n{name} / {mode}\n', file=disassembly)
+            dis.dis(fn, file=disassembly, show_caches=True)
+        for _ in range(args.rounds):
+            order = modes.copy()
+            rng.shuffle(order)
+            for mode in order:
+                timer, count = timers[mode]
+                elapsed = timer.timeit(count)
+                case['modes'][mode]['samples_ns'].append(elapsed * 1e9 / count / args.iterations)
+        for mode, data in case['modes'].items():
+            data['median_ns'] = statistics.median(data['samples_ns'])
+            data['mad_ns'] = statistics.median(abs(s - data['median_ns']) for s in data['samples_ns'])
+        plain = case['modes']['plain']['median_ns']
+        for data in case['modes'].values():
+            data['relative_time'] = data['median_ns'] / plain
+        print(name, {mode: round(data['relative_time'], 3)
+                     for mode, data in case['modes'].items()}, flush=True)
+        (args.output / 'benchmark.json').write_text(json.dumps(results, indent=2))
+    (args.output / 'disassembly.txt').write_text(disassembly.getvalue())
+    # Separate exception creation/capture from traceback formatting.
+    failure = results['failure'] = {}
+    for mode in modes:
+        fn = build('a == b', mode, rewrite)
+        def catch():
+            try:
+                fn(1, 2)
+            except AssertionError:
+                pass
+        def display():
+            try:
+                fn(1, 2)
+            except AssertionError as exc:
+                traceback.format_exception(exc)
+        failure[mode] = {}
+        for label, action in [('raise_catch', catch), ('raise_format', display)]:
+            module_loaded = 'assertionlib' in sys.modules
+            timer = timeit.Timer(action)
+            loops = calibrate(timer, args.sample_seconds)
+            samples = [timer.timeit(loops) * 1e9 / loops for _ in range(args.rounds)]
+            failure[mode][label] = {
+                'assertionlib_loaded_before': module_loaded,
+                'samples_ns': samples, 'median_ns': statistics.median(samples),
+            }
+    if args.callbacks:
+        import assertionlib
+        callback_results = results['callbacks'] = {}
+        failed_fn = build('a == b', 'native')
+        def failed():
+            try:
+                failed_fn(1, 2)
+            except AssertionError:
+                pass
+        cases = [
+            ('failure_no_handler', failed, {}),
+            ('failure_handler', failed, {'on_failure': lambda error, details: None}),
+            ('failure_format_handler', failed,
+             {'on_failure': lambda error, details: assertionlib.format_details(details)}),
+        ]
+        for name, action, handlers in cases:
+            with assertionlib.handlers(**handlers):
+                timer = timeit.Timer(action)
+                count = calibrate(timer, args.sample_seconds)
+                samples = [timer.timeit(count) * 1e9 / count for _ in range(args.rounds)]
+            callback_results[name] = {
+                'samples_ns': samples, 'median_ns': statistics.median(samples),
+            }
+    (args.output / 'benchmark.json').write_text(json.dumps(results, indent=2))
+
+
+if __name__ == '__main__':
+    main()
